1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,227
This is a cash-on-delivery checkout.
The customer replaced her SIM this
week.

2
00:00:05,227 --> 00:00:13,397
A fake order costs a merchant a real
delivery run. Automatically declining
a legitimate customer loses the sale.

3
00:00:13,397 --> 00:00:22,558
A SIM change on its own does not tell
those two apart: a takeover, and a
replaced lost phone, produce the same
signal.

4
00:00:23,408 --> 00:00:26,907
The agent picks each check
itself, and shows you why.

5
00:00:26,907 --> 00:00:32,283
Number verification first: the cheapest
useful check, and the network number
matches.

6
00:00:32,283 --> 00:00:36,997
Then SIM swap. It comes back
adverse, and it stays in the
chain.

7
00:00:36,997 --> 00:00:42,309
The operator's change date is a separate
row, because it is a second billable call.

8
00:00:42,309 --> 00:00:47,728
Device swap: the handset is unchanged.
Location: she is where she said she is.

9
00:00:47,728 --> 00:00:55,067
Watch the label on each row. This
run uses the deterministic
planner, so every selection reads
greedy,

10
00:00:55,067 --> 00:00:58,267
with the reason it won, and
the budget left after it.

11
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:05,883
Under the Gemini planner the same rows
name the model where it chose, and
policy where a check could not be
waived.

12
00:01:05,883 --> 00:01:10,905
The planner decides what to buy.
Policy decides what the answer
means.

13
00:01:11,755 --> 00:01:13,525
Challenge. Not a decline.

14
00:01:13,525 --> 00:01:17,216
The adverse fact is still there,
and three checks corroborate her,

15
00:01:17,216 --> 00:01:23,125
so the merchant asks for their
own step-up instead of turning
away a customer who lost her
phone.

16
00:01:23,125 --> 00:01:29,333
Isnad did not send a one-time code. The
merchant runs that, in a channel we do not
own.

17
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:38,153
The score is 0.322: an
uncalibrated policy score, not a
fraud probability, and the page
says so.

18
00:01:39,003 --> 00:01:43,739
This is the signed receipt. The
signature is over the exact stored
bytes.

19
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:46,661
Change one character,
and the signature
fails.

20
00:01:46,661 --> 00:01:51,269
What that proves is what Isnad issued,
and that it has not been altered since.

21
00:01:51,269 --> 00:01:54,256
It does not prove the
operator told us the
truth.

22
00:01:54,256 --> 00:02:00,976
Every row in this run is a
simulated operator answer, and
each one says so on its own
badge.

23
00:02:00,976 --> 00:02:04,227
A signed mock receipt
is still a mock
receipt.

24
00:02:05,077 --> 00:02:11,627
A judge can call the API
directly: the organizer's code
mints a merchant key, shown
once.

25
00:02:11,627 --> 00:02:18,270
The same request from a terminal
returns the same signed verdict,
from the mock, never from Nokia.

26
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:24,389
The same engine against a run-everything
pipeline, on a thirteen-case authored set:

27
00:02:24,389 --> 00:02:28,080
forty-nine provider
operations against
seventy-eight.

28
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:35,120
A count of calls, not money: the
cost figures are the policy's
budget units, not an operator's
prices.

29
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:39,771
Eight CAMARA APIs are
integrated on Nokia Network
as Code,

30
00:02:39,771 --> 00:02:46,469
and the recorded calls to Nokia's
hosted simulator are in the
repository with their failures
intact.

31
00:02:46,469 --> 00:02:52,251
What we have not proven, we
name: no physical handset has
completed a consent round
trip,

32
00:02:52,251 --> 00:02:54,533
and no merchant outcomes have been measured.
